25 (a leap day every 4 years), but that is slightly TOO LONG, gaining ~3 days per 400 years, so by 1582 it had drifted ~10 days (the equinox slipping from ~March 21 to ~March 11)", "The Julian calendar had too few leap years, so it fell behind", "The year is exactly 365.25 days, so the Julian calendar was perfect"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The tropical year (equinox to equinox, governing the seasons) is ~365.2422 days, and that stubborn fraction forces any whole-number calendar to drift unless corrected. Caesar's Julian calendar used 365.25 (leap every 4 years) \u2014 close, but too long by ~0.0078 day/year, which accumulates to ~3 days per 400 years. By 1582 the Julian calendar had gained ~10 days on the seasons, dragging the vernal equinox from March 21 (its Nicaea date) to about March 11 \u2014 a real problem for a civilisation that set Easter by the equinox."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "How did the Gregorian reform fix the calendar, and why was 1900 a common year but 2000 a leap year?", "options": ["It added a leap day every year; 2000 was special because it was round", "It deleted 10 accumulated days (Oct 4 \u2192 Oct 15, 1582) to reset the equinox, AND refined the leap rule: leap every 4 years EXCEPT century years, which are leap only if divisible by 400 \u2014 so 1900 (not \u00f7400) was skipped but 2000 (\u00f7400) kept, removing 3 leap-days per 400 years and trimming the average year to 365.2425 days", "It made every century year a leap year; 1900 was an error", "It removed leap years entirely, which is why 1900 wasn't one"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Gregory did two things in 1582: corrected the accumulated ~10-day error by deleting ten days (October 4 was followed by October 15), snapping the equinox back to March 21; and refined the leap-year rule to prevent future drift \u2014 leap year every 4 years, except century years, which are leap only if divisible by 400. This skips 3 leap-days per 400 years (1700, 1800, 1900 skipped; 1600, 2000 kept), giving 97 leap-days per 400 years and an average year of 365.2425 days \u2014 off the true 365.2422 by ~26 seconds, or one day in ~3,300 years."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "Explain why a 'day' is really two things \u2014 the solar day and the sidereal day \u2014 including their lengths, the geometric reason they differ (connecting to the two months), and the visible consequence for the night sky. Then explain why we keep 'mean' time and standardised time zones rather than the real Sun's local time.", "rubric": "THE TWO DAYS: the SOLAR day is noon to noon (Sun due south to due south again), measured relative to the SUN \u2014 24 HOURS by definition (what our clocks/civil day are built on). The SIDEREAL day is measured relative to the STARS (a given star returning to the same position) \u2014 Earth's TRUE rotation \u2014 about 23h 56m (~4 minutes SHORTER). WHY THEY DIFFER (the geometry, = the two-months logic): the sidereal day is one true spin relative to the fixed stars. But the solar day is measured against the Sun, and while the Earth spins it is ALSO moving along its ORBIT, covering ~1\u00b0 per day (360\u00b0 in ~365 days). So after one full rotation relative to the stars, the Sun is NOT yet back to noon \u2014 the Earth has slid ~1\u00b0 around its orbit, shifting the Sun's direction \u2014 and the Earth must turn an EXTRA ~1\u00b0 to bring the Sun due south again. At 15\u00b0/hour, that extra 1\u00b0 takes ~4 MINUTES, so the solar day is ~4 min LONGER. This is EXACTLY the 'chasing the moving Sun' logic that made the SYNODIC month longer than the SIDEREAL month: measuring rotation against a MOVING reference (the Sun) vs a FIXED one (the stars). VISIBLE CONSEQUENCE: because the sidereal day (governing when STARS return) is ~4 min shorter than the solar day (our clocks), a given star rises ~4 MINUTES EARLIER each night \u2014 ~half an hour a week, ~2 hours a month \u2014 and over a year 4 min \u00d7 365 \u2248 24 hours, a FULL cycle. So different constellations fill the EVENING sky in different SEASONS (Orion on winter evenings, Scorpius on summer ones), the whole zodiac wheeling through the year. The changing seasonal sky IS the solar\u2013sidereal gap, the daily accounting of Earth's ORBIT. MEAN TIME (taming the uneven Sun): the APPARENT solar day (real Sun) is NOT exactly 24 hours every day \u2014 it varies because (1) Earth's orbit is ELLIPTICAL (faster near perihelion, slower near aphelion, so the ~4-min correction varies) and (2) the Sun moves along the TILTED ecliptic (its eastward progress varies with season). Together these make the real Sun run ahead/behind a uniform clock by up to ~16 minutes \u2014 the EQUATION OF TIME (same effect as the analemma's east\u2013west width). Since clocks can't speed up and slow down, we keep MEAN TIME by a fictitious uniform 'mean Sun' (24-hour days averaged); a sundial keeps APPARENT time, so it and your watch disagree by up to 16 min. TIME ZONES: even mean LOCAL time depends on your exact longitude (local noon differs 4 min/degree), so every town once kept its own clock \u2014 chaos once railways/telegraphs linked places. TIME ZONES divide the world into broad bands each keeping one STANDARD time (anchored to GREENWICH), uniform across whole regions. A strong answer notes the tidy time on your phone descends from astronomy \u2014 the tilted, elliptical orbit and the 15\u00b0/hour spin \u2014 averaged and standardised."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "Lay out the calendar problem and its solution: why the year cannot fit a whole-number calendar, the Julian calendar and its flaw, and the Gregorian reform (both what it did to the accumulated error and its refined leap-year rule) \u2014 and explain precisely why 1700/1800/1900 were common years but 1600/2000 were leap years, and how accurate the result is.", "rubric": "THE PROBLEM: the TROPICAL year \u2014 equinox to equinox, which governs the SEASONS \u2014 is ~365.2422 days, NOT a whole number (365 + ~0.2422 day \u2248 365 days + ~5h 49m). A workable calendar needs a WHOLE number of days per year (you can't end a year partway through a day). So the extra ~0.2422 day per year ACCUMULATES, and a whole-number calendar inevitably DRIFTS against the seasons (eventually the calendar says 'summer' in winter) unless corrected. The art of calendar-making is managing that drift. THE JULIAN FIX + FLAW: Julius Caesar (46 BC) approximated the year as 365.25 days by adding a LEAP DAY every 4 years (Feb 29) \u2014 three 365-day years + one 366 averages 365.25, far better than a flat 365, and it served ~1,600 years. But 365.25 is TOO LONG by 0.0078 day/year (~11 min), which accumulates to ~3 extra days per 400 years. By 1582 the Julian calendar had gained ~10 DAYS on the seasons, dragging the vernal equinox from ~March 21 (its date at the Council of Nicaea, 325 AD) to ~March 11 \u2014 a real problem, since Easter was set by the equinox. THE GREGORIAN REFORM (1582, Pope Gregory XIII): (1) CORRECTED the accumulated error by DELETING 10 DAYS \u2014 October 4 was followed directly by October 15, 1582 \u2014 snapping the equinox back to ~March 21; (2) REFINED the leap-year rule to stop future drift: a year is leap if divisible by 4, EXCEPT century years, which are leap ONLY if divisible by 400. WHY 1700/1800/1900 vs 1600/2000: century years are divisible by 100; under the new rule they are leap ONLY if also divisible by 400. 1700, 1800, 1900 are NOT divisible by 400 \u2192 SKIPPED (common years, 365 days); 1600 and 2000 ARE divisible by 400 \u2192 KEPT (leap years). This REMOVES 3 leap-days every 400 years (the 3 skipped centuries). ACCURACY: that gives 97 leap-days per 400 years, so the average year = 365 + 97/400 = 365.2425 days \u2014 compared to the true 365.2422, an error of only ~0.0003 day/year (~26 seconds), i.e. ONE day's drift in ~3,300 years \u2014 excellent. THE LESSON: the odd century-year rule is NOT bureaucratic fussiness but a finely tuned solution to the year being 365.2422 days; the calendar is at bottom a SHADOW OF A GEOMETRY \u2014 Earth's orbit, whose period refuses to be a whole number of Earth's rotations. A strong answer frames the whole thing as precise astronomy forcing practical precision, with the leap-century twist as the elegant patch."}]$astroT_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
insert into quizzes (node_id, level, questions, generated)
select 'astronomy-and-space/foundations/observed-sky/timekeeping', lvl, q.questions, true
from q, (values ('easy'), ('advanced'), ('expert')) as l(lvl)
on conflict (node_id, level) do update
  set questions = excluded.questions, generated = excluded.generated;
